{"id":"hospice-state-utilization","title":"Hospices: patients who leave alive, very long stays, fast growth and missing State reports","question":"Which San Francisco and Bay Area hospices report unusually many patients leaving hospice alive or staying past six months, grew fastest, or stopped filing their State utilization report?","finding":"The State utilization files list 12 San Francisco hospices; 2 meet a test. Across the Bay Area 28 more do. In California the middle hospice discharged 48% of its patients alive in 2025 (35% in 2019) and 23% after 180 days or more. 9 Bay Area hospices filed no State report in two or more of the last three years; Medicare paid them $3.1 million in 2024.","how":"From the State's Home Health Agency and Hospice Annual Utilization Data, report years 2019 to 2025 (filed reports and the non-response list in each workbook). Live discharges = total discharges by diagnosis less deaths; long stays = discharges after 180 days or more over all discharges by length of stay; both only when a hospice had 20 or more discharges. A hospice is listed when (with 30 or more discharges) its live-discharge or long-stay share is at least 1.5 times the California middle that year, its patients grew at least five-fold to 100 or more, or it is on the non-response list in two of the last three years. San Francisco hospices are all listed. Medicare payment for 2024 comes from the federal post-acute provider file, linked to the State facility number by the earlier pull.","limits":"Patients with dementia or heart failure live longer and are more often discharged alive; a hospice serving nursing-home residents or opening branches grows fast; a small agency's shares swing on a few patients. A missing State report is a breach of a reporting duty, not proof of anything else; some agencies on the list had closed or changed name. The State files count patients, not people across agencies, and nobody is named.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"hospice","t":"Hospice